.TH IV 1 "September 2026" "iv 0.11.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
iv \- line-oriented text editor for scripts and pipes
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B iv
.B \-v
.RI [ \-\-no\-numbers ]
.IR file
.PP
.B iv
.B \-va
.RI [ \-\-no\-numbers ]
.IR start\-end
.IR file
.PP
.B iv
.B \-wc
.IR file
.PP
.B iv
.B \-n
.IR file
.IR pattern
.RI [ \-\-json ]
.PP
.B iv
.B \-nv
.RI [ \-\-no\-numbers ]
.IR file
.IR pattern
.PP
.B iv
.BR "\-i," " \-\-insert"
.IR file
.RI [ start\-end ]
.IR "text"
.RI [ \-q ]
.RI [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.PP
.B iv
.B \-a
.IR file
.IR "text"
.RI [ \-q ]
.PP
.B iv
.B \-p
.IR file
.RI [ file... ]
.RI [ range ]
.IR content
.RI [ \-q ]
.PP
.B iv
.B \-pi
.IR file
.RI [ file... ]
.IR line
.IR content
.RI [ \-q ]
.PP
.B iv
.BR "\-d," " \-\-delete"
.IR file
.RI [ start\-end ]
.RI [ \-m
.IR pattern ]
.RI [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.PP
.B iv
.BR "\-r," " \-\-replace"
.IR file
.RI [ start\-end ]
.IR "text"
.RI [ \-m
.IR pattern ]
.RI [ \-q ]
.RI [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.PP
.B iv
.B \-s
.IR file
.IR pattern
.IR replacement
.RI [ \-e
.IR pattern
.IR replacement ]
.RI [ \-m
.IR pattern ]
.RI [ \-F
.IR delim
.IR N
.IR value ]
.RI [ \-E ]
.RI [ \-g ]
.RI [ \-q ]
.RI [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.PP
.B iv
.BR "\-h," " \-\-help"
.PP
.B iv
.BR "\-V," " \-\-version"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B iv
is a line-oriented text editor for the command line.
Runtime depends only on libc. Edits compose with pipes.
.B \-\-help ,
.B \-\-version
and diagnostics are English. A Spanish page is
.B man \-L es iv
when installed. Distros translate manuals, not
.BR \-\-version .
.PP
Text arguments accept three forms:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-
reads from stdin
.IP \(bu 2
path to existing file
reads file content
.IP \(bu 2
any other text
used as literal
.RE
.SH VIEW COMMANDS
.TP
.B \-v
View entire file with line numbers.
.TP
.B \-va
View line range. Order: \fB\-va\fR \fIstart\-end\fR \fIfile\fR.
.TP
.B \-wc
Count lines.
.TP
.B \-n
Print line numbers where \fIpattern\fR appears.
.B \-\-json
outputs {"lines":[1,5,7]} for jq/Python integration.
.TP
.B \-nv
Print matching lines where \fIpattern\fR appears.
By default prints with line numbers; use \fB\-\-no\-numbers\fR to print only the line contents.
.TP
.SH EDIT COMMANDS
.TP
.B \-i
.B \-\-insert
Insert text. Without range, appends at end.
.TP
.B \-a
Append text at end of file.
.TP
.B \-p
Patch one or more files. Optional range for replace mode.
.TP
.B \-pi
Patch insert: insert a new line \fIbefore\fR the given line without replacing it;
the line and all following lines are shifted down. Requires \fIline\fR (e.g. 1).
Without line, appends at end. Use to insert e.g. an include at line 1.
.TP
.B \-d
.B \-\-delete
Delete lines. With
.B \-m
\fIpattern\fR, delete only matching lines.
.TP
.B \-r
.B \-\-replace
Replace lines. With
.B \-m
\fIpattern\fR, replace only matching lines.
.TP
.B \-s
Search and replace.
.B \-e
adds more replacements (like sed \-e).
.B \-m
\fIpattern\fR restricts to lines containing pattern.
.B \-F
\fIdelim\fR \fIN\fR \fIvalue\fR replaces field N (delimiter-separated text, not CSV quoting).
.B \-E
.B \-\-regex
enable POSIX ERE. Replacement supports \fB\\1\fR–\fB\\9\fR and \fB&\fR.
An empty pattern is rejected.
\fB\-g\fR replaces all matches per line.
.B \-m
uses the same language as the substitute (literal, or ERE with \fB\-E\fR).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Show what would be done without modifying files.
.TP
.B \-b
GNU backup with method
.BR existing .
.TP
.B \-\-backup[=METHOD]
GNU backup. Without METHOD, use
.B VERSION_CONTROL
or
.BR existing .
Methods (unique abbreviations):
.BR none / off ,
.BR numbered / t ,
.BR existing / nil ,
.BR simple / never .
.TP
.B \-S SUFFIX
.B \-\-suffix=SUFFIX
Append SUFFIX to the backup name (default
.B SIMPLE_BACKUP_SUFFIX
or
.BR ~ ).
Also enables backup.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-numbers
Omit line numbers in \-v and \-va output.
.TP
.B \-q
Suppress tee-like output (\-i, \-a, \-r, \-p, \-pi) and the
.B Replaced N
line on
.BR \-s .
.TP
.B \-\-stdout
Write result to stdout instead of modifying file.
.TP
.B \-E
.B \-\-regex
POSIX ERE for
.BR \-s ,
.BR \-m ,
.B \-n
and
.BR \-nv .
.SH RANGES
1-based. Examples:
.RS
.IP \fB1\-5\fR 8
lines 1 through 5
.IP \fB5\fR 8
line 5 only
.IP \fB\-3\fR 8
third line from end
.IP \fB\-3\-\-1\fR 8
last three lines
.IP \fB\-5\-\fR 8
last five lines
.IP \fB2\-\fR 8
from line 2 to end
.RE
.SH ESCAPE SEQUENCES
In insert/replace text: \fB\\n\fR newline, \fB\\t\fR tab, \fB\\\\\fR backslash, \fB\\r\fR carriage return.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B VERSION_CONTROL
Default method for
.B \-\-backup
and
.B \-S
when no method is given.
.TP
.B SIMPLE_BACKUP_SUFFIX
Suffix for simple backups when
.B \-S
is not used. Default
.BR ~ .
.PP
Backup names sit next to the file:
.I file~
or
.IR file.~N~ .
Restore with
.BR mv (1);
compare with
.BR diff (1).
.SH FILE WRITES
.SH FILE WRITES
In-place edits open the parent directory, create an exclusive temp via \fBopenat\fR(2),
\fBfsync\fR it, check that the destination inode is unchanged, then \fBrenameat\fR(2).
A failed edit leaves the original path unchanged.
Dangling symbolic links are rejected; they are never replaced by a regular file.
Symbolic links: the referent is replaced; the symlink inode is kept.
Hard links: this pathname gets a new inode; other names keep the old bytes.
Mode and owner are copied when permitted; xattrs and ACLs are not.
\fBSIGINT\fR/\fBSIGTERM\fR/\fBSIGHUP\fR unlink a leftover \fB.iv.*\fR temp.
A requested backup is a copy of the original taken immediately before the rename.
This is not a security boundary against a hostile writable directory.
.SH BYTES AND LOCALE
iv is byte-oriented. It runs in the C locale.
A line is bytes ending in \fB\\n\fR or EOF. Embedded NUL is binary and refused.
An empty substitute pattern is rejected.
Directories, FIFOs and devices are rejected; only regular files are edited.
\fB\-F\fR takes a single delimiter byte (the first byte of the argument).
POSIX ERE (\fB\-E\fR) is compiled and matched as C-locale bytes.
Invalid UTF-8 is treated as bytes, not repaired.
.SH PIPELINES
Stdout \fBEPIPE\fR (downstream closed) is success: exit 0, no diagnostic.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success (including stdout closed by a downstream filter)
.TP
.B 1
Error (binary file, invalid range, write failure, usage error)
